A||||14||N_Man||ATTN: suspension guys||||1||
Z||000000||N_Man||12-01-2000||08:22 PM||||Got a few questions about sway bars and wheels.  First of all i want to make my car handle pretty neutral, if anything i want a little bit of understeer.<P>So first off i was thinking of a fatter front sway bar and putting the front bar on the rear(first concern).  Im concearned with the weight ditribution.  The F. swaybar mounted on the rear is behind the rear axle. proper aftermaket ones put the weight infront of the rear axle.<BR>So my BIG question is how much will the weight ditribution be effected by this.<P>Secondly i was looking at wheels and im not sure between 16's or 17's heading more towards the 16's.  Two reasons for the 16's lighter and cheaper.  (suggestion for LIGHT wheels?)<BR>On a 16x7.5 rim i was thinking 225/50rear and 215/50front.<P>Of course im forgetting lowering springs possibly the eibach set.<P>In the end i want almost neutral handling with just alittle bit of understeer so any suggestions will be welcomed.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Will||12-02-2000||12:26 AM||||The position of a sway-bar will have exactly zero effect on weight distribution. Even the stock rear bar on '88's is mounted behind the axle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jerpro||12-02-2000||04:12 PM||||like i have already posted,<BR>check this link <A HREF="http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/dynamics102.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/dynamics102.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000003||PearlBlueSoul||12-02-2000||05:14 PM||||If you want to adjust your handling that much, get Suspension Technique's sway bar kit they make.  It's adjustable, and you can tune it any way you want- oversteer, neutral, or understeer in each extreme.  The polyurethane bushing replacements are a plus and made a HUGE difference in handling.  most bang-for-the-buck mod I've done yet.<P>Wanna know how I know they work?  At Dragon Run 2k (MR2 meet last october) there was a guy there that had the same sway bar kit I did.  He had his set wrong however, and he understeered like MAD.  Just taking a U turn made it understeer.  so I know it can be tuned to cause any extreme you want.  The way I have mine set cancels out Toyota's stock handling attribute of initial understeer transitioning into oversteer.  With the bars, I instead get neutral to transitional oversteer (very predictable still too, the way the stock setup normally acted).  <P>The kit direct from nopi shipped to your front door was $255 front and rear with all of the neccesary extra hardware I needed (rear brackets and end links for the rear bar my car didn't come with).  So, there's no need to go out to the parts store for extra parts.  All in all, it was around a 1 hour job on my car to install, not hard at all.  Just an unbolt and rebolt affair.  <P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">         <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.<p>[This message has been edited by PearlBlueSoul (edited 12-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||PearlBlueSoul||12-02-2000||05:43 PM||||OK, I'm an idiot.  <P>Didn't know that ST doesn't make bars for Fieros...least no one I can find carries them.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  someone smack me when they see me.<P>I don't know if someone does make a set like the ST bars, but if so I would suggest getting those.  <P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000005||N_Man||12-02-2000||07:55 PM||||Jerpro<BR>that is a great site, things i sorta knew became much clearer.<P>Will<BR>Why wouldnt position of the sway bar have an effect on the wieght ditribution?  If i add 500 pounds to the very middle of a car the weight will increase by 500 pounds but weight distrobution will stay the same.  If i added this same 500 pounds closer to one axle or the other im gonna change the weight distrobution.  If i add this weight on the far side of an axle then the weight I add will have an even greater effect on the balance because the axle will act like a pivot in a cantilever.  so it EFFECTIVLY adds more weight to the rear end then I actually add. bla bla bla<BR>Now at the same time I know i very well might be wrong about the physics, if i am please explain how.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||12-03-2000||12:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by N_Man:<BR><B>Jerpro<BR>that is a great site, things i sorta knew became much clearer.<P>Will<BR>Why wouldnt position of the sway bar have an effect on the wieght ditribution?  If i add 500 pounds to the very middle of a car the weight will increase by 500 pounds but weight distrobution will stay the same.  If i added this same 500 pounds closer to one axle or the other im gonna change the weight distrobution.  If i add this weight on the far side of an axle then the weight I add will have an even greater effect on the balance because the axle will act like a pivot in a cantilever.  so it EFFECTIVLY adds more weight to the rear end then I actually add. bla bla bla<BR>Now at the same time I know i very well might be wrong about the physics, if i am please explain how.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>An anti-sway bar weighs less than 20 lbs. It won't matter.<BR>For the record, my DIY front to rear sway bar swap is in front of the rear axle, but only because the cradle rails were closer together there. The bar also helps protect the oil pan in that location<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||N_Man||12-03-2000||05:10 PM||||So could anybody answer this:  In a fiero how much weight, gained or lost, on the axle would it take to change the weight distrobution 1%?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Got2manyfieros||12-03-2000||08:15 PM||||If you are so concerned about weight distribution, you should consider moving the battery to the front of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||12-04-2000||11:30 AM||||My car weighs 2750. If it had 50/50 distribution, there would be 1375 on each end. 1402.5 lbs rear and 1347.5 front would give 49/51 distribution. The math isn't hard. Multiplying the weight percentage on one axle by the weight of your car will give the weight on that axle.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tigger||12-04-2000||11:52 AM||||I'm think'in the advantages of adding a rear sway bar would outweigh any concern for weight distribution.  Yea, you'll be adding 20 lbs or so in the rear, but the biggest gain will be in handling.  If you're adding a bigger bar in the front, which will likely be slightly more weight, then your distribution change should be minimal.<P>16's are definitely cheaper.  For acceleration, less wheel mass is key.  For handling, lighter wheel = less unsprung mass = better handling.  Only advantage I can see of larger wheel dia's, other than looks, are for overall top speed, 140 or above.  If you can find lightweight 17's, you should have no problems.<P>Just my thoughts<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||12-04-2000||12:41 PM||||The idea behind large wheels is that they wear low profile tires with short stiff sidewalls that deflect less under cornering than the 60 series tires Fieros wear stock. Lighter wheels = better handling isn't the only side of the coin. Lighter wheels can improve transient response, but you may lose stead state cornering power (the kind of cornering you do in a long sweeping turn like a merge ramp) by going to a taller, mushier sidewall. The fact that larger wheels have larger moments of inertia and are harder to accelerate simply spawns a design compromise that must be optimised. If you were driving a race car the answer would be simple: whatever lets you get the lowest lap time.<BR>Since this is (I assume) as street car, there are other compromises to attend: shorter sidewalls will give a rougher ride, and provide less protection for your wheels from deep potholes and the like.<BR>     In the end, expect to do a lot of good old fashioned trial and error tuning to get it right. It's not a quick and easy question that you can look up somewhere on the internet. You can get close with other people's advice, but in the end YOU are driving the car and YOU must make the final adjustments to get it exactly the way YOU want it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||N_Man||12-04-2000||01:00 PM||||yep in the end it has to be how i want it.  But getting the best starting point is what im after here.<BR>The 16's are the best compromise for me.  Good handling better ride and lighter weight.  After putting my winter wheels and tires on i NEED to have light weight.<BR>The car is a daily driver that should see a race trak maybe three or four times in a year.  So I dont want to be bounced out of my seat.<BR>So looking at you math Will moving the battery up front should be helpfull.  Just somthing else to consider||||reg||1
